{"reference":"Isaiah 11","book":"Isaiah","chapter":11,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Interpretive Questions","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"Isaiah 11 uses royal and Davidic language to picture hoped-for just rule. Its first historical referent, date, and relationship to later messianic readings remain disputed. Careful reading distinguishes the poem’s literary sense and possible historical setting from later claims of prediction, typology, quotation, or broader canonical interpretation.","verse_refs":["Isaiah 11:1-16"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_isaiah_011_42d35feaa7c757339edcff87"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"},{"id":"block_2","text":"Verse 4’s image of defeating the wicked “with the breath of his lips” is plausibly echoed in a later description of the Lord defeating a lawless opponent with the breath of his mouth. The connection is interpretive rather than certain, but it highlights how Isaiah’s image of just rule also includes the removal of wickedness.","verse_refs":["Isaiah 11:4"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_isaiah_011_376e660509875fcf9161ae21"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"},{"id":"block_3","text":"The picture in verse 9 of the earth filled with knowledge of Jehovah “as the waters cover the sea” resembles Habakkuk’s contrast between futile imperial labor and the earth being filled with knowledge of Jehovah’s glory. The comparison places Isaiah’s vision of peace alongside a vision in which divine knowledge displaces futile human power.","verse_refs":["Isaiah 11:9"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_isaiah_011_71903f29cf93e739dc3063b0"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"},{"id":"block_4","text":"Verse 5 portrays righteousness and faithfulness as the ruler’s girdle, clothing that marks and secures his rule. A later armor passage reuses scriptural divine-warrior and peace imagery, culminating repeatedly in prayer and truthful witness; the relationship between these texts is interpretive rather than settled.","verse_refs":["Isaiah 11:5"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_isaiah_011_74ffa7153d25f09fb87ab2aa"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"Surrounding Passages","blocks":[{"id":"block_5","text":"Isaiah is preserved in the Great Isaiah Scroll, an almost complete witness to all sixty-six chapters. It broadly aligns with the Masoretic tradition while also containing many spelling and substantive differences; other Qumran Isaiah manuscripts and interpretive writings show that the book was actively copied and interpreted.","provenance_refs":["reader_path_isaiah_011_eb219cb54f9da054d828c3c8"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}